==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 05 — NMAP PART 2
Scan Types, Timing & Port Ranges
==========================================

--- SCAN TYPES ---
sudo nmap -sS <target>        # SYN scan ("half-open") - default when run as root, stealthier
nmap -sT <target>              # TCP Connect scan - full handshake, no root needed
sudo nmap -sU <target>          # UDP scan - different protocol, much slower, less certain results
sudo nmap -sU -p 53,161 <target> # UDP scan limited to specific ports (recommended - full UDP is very slow)

--- TIMING TEMPLATES (speed vs stealth tradeoff) ---
nmap -T0 <target>    # paranoid - extremely slow, for evading detection
nmap -T1 <target>    # sneaky - very slow
nmap -T2 <target>    # polite - slower, less bandwidth/load on target
nmap -T3 <target>    # normal - the DEFAULT if you don't specify
nmap -T4 <target>    # aggressive - faster, assumes a reliable fast network
nmap -T5 <target>    # insane - fastest, may sacrifice accuracy

--- MEASURING ACTUAL SCAN TIME ---
time nmap -T2 -F <target>       # 'time' prefix shows real/user/sys time taken

--- PORT SPECIFICATION ---
nmap -p 22 <target>                  # single port
nmap -p 21,22,25,80,443 <target>      # specific list
nmap -p 1-100 <target>                 # range
nmap -p 1-50,80,443,8080 <target>       # range + list combined
nmap -p- <target>                        # ALL 65535 ports
nmap --top-ports 20 <target>              # just the top N most common ports
nmap -F <target>                           # shortcut for --top-ports 100

--- SCAN TYPE COMPARISON ---
-sS   SYN scan       needs root, stealthier (never completes TCP handshake)
-sT   Connect scan   no root needed, completes full handshake (more "visible")
-sU   UDP scan       slower, results often "open|filtered" (less certain than TCP)

--- UDP RESULT STATES (different from TCP) ---
open           -> got an actual response back
open|filtered  -> no response at all; could be open with a silent service, or filtered - can't tell
closed         -> got an ICMP "port unreachable" message back

